{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Final rebar-debug repair report.\n\nProblem — repo-isolation session hooks could keep pytest alive indefinitely after the test body completed when a read-only Git probe blocked.\n\nProven root cause — tests/_isolation.py invoked both `git rev-parse HEAD` and `git status --porcelain` without a subprocess timeout; Phase 1 runtime instrumentation showed the process blocked in the real post-test hook boundary. Ordinary probe failures were intentionally collapsed to `None`, but a hung child never returned to that handler.\n\nProof — a PATH-injected Git shim blocked only the second real guard probe. Without the repair, both real hook-path tests exceeded their 12-second outer hang guard and failed; normal Git controls completed. This distinguishes the missing inner watchdog from test-body hangs or pytest teardown ordering.\n\nTest — tests/unit/test_repo_isolation_guard.py drives `porcelain()` through `pytest_sessionfinish` and `head()` through the autouse commit guard. The final oracle was GREEN (11/11 focused tests). Exact-defect mutation removing both inner timeout arguments returned the two hook tests to RED (0 passed, 2 failed after their outer guards); restoring the repair returned them to GREEN (2/2). The intentional wall-clock ceiling is explicitly sanctioned as a hang guard, and scripts/check_wall_clock_asserts.py is GREEN.\n\nFix — both read-only Git probes now use the fixed five-second `GIT_PROBE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` watchdog and explicitly re-raise `subprocess.TimeoutExpired`, while preserving `None` for ordinary OSError/SubprocessError/non-repository failures.\n\nScope and safety — the change is confined to test infrastructure and its regression tests; production behavior and dependencies are unchanged. Existing assertions were strengthened, not weakened. Final validation: focused tests pass, `make lint` passes, `make typecheck` passes, wall-clock-assert gate passes, and `git diff --check` is clean.\n\nSiblings — both live repo-isolation Git probe surfaces (`head` and `porcelain`) were fixed and exercised through their distinct real hook callers. No other subprocess probe exists in tests/_isolation.py. CLI/MCP/library parity is N/A because this is pytest-only infrastructure.\n\nResidual risk and prevention — the outer 12-second subprocess ceiling remains only as a stuck-test fail-safe and is now annotated for the repository timing gate; accepted because it does not determine functional success.\n\nReplication — the original blocked-second-probe scenario now terminates through the five-second inner watchdog with an explicit isolation-infrastructure failure rather than hanging pytest."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787072041574238001,"uuid":"bfdb4aca-2f6e-4c9b-962f-11487f9217f3"}